Suppose that $Y$ is a random vector having values in $(-l, \infty)^n$, where $l \geq 0$. Let $\Phi: (-l \sqrt{n}, \infty) \to \mathbb{R}$ be continuous and assume that there exists a measurable function $M:(-l,\infty)^n \to \mathbb{R}$, such that one has $\sup_{x \in S_+^{n-1}}|\Phi(\left\langle x, y\right\rangle)| \leq M(y)$ and $\mathbb{E} M(Y)<\infty$. Then, the function $F:S_+^{n-1} \to \mathbb{R}$ defined by $F(x) = \mathbb{E} \Phi(\left\langle x, Y\right\rangle)$ is continuous.

Suppose that $Y=(Y_1, \ldots, Y_n)$, where $Y_1, \ldots, Y_n$ are i.i.d. random variables having values in the interval $(-l, \infty)$, where $l \geq 0$. Let $\Phi: (-l \sqrt{n}, \infty) \to \mathbb{R}$ be such that the function $F(x)=\mathbb{E} \Phi(\left\langle x, Y\right\rangle)$ is continuous. Suppose that for every $0 \leq d_1 < d_2$ satisfying $d_1^2+d_2^2 \leq 1$  we have
\begin{equation}
\mathbb{E} \Phi(s+d_1 Y_1 + d_2Y_2) \leq \mathbb{E} \Phi\left(s+\sqrt{\frac12(d_1^2+d_2^2)}(Y_1 + Y_2) \right), 
\end{equation} 
for every  $s \geq - l \sqrt{(n-2)(1-d_1^2-d_2^2)}$. Then,
\begin{equation}
\mathbb{E} \Phi\left(\sum_{i=1}^n d_i Y_i \right) \leq \mathbb{E} \Phi\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{n}}\sum_{i=1}^n Y_i \right)
\end{equation}
for all $(d_1,\ldots, d_n) \in S_+^{n-1}$. Moreover, for $(d_1, \ldots, d_n) \ne (n^{-1/2},\ldots, n^{-1/2})$, if (2) is always strict, then the inequality (3) is strict.

Suppose $p,q$ are probability densities of random variables $U$ and $V$, respectively. Take $\Phi=-\ln q$. Then 
\begin{itemize}
	\item[(a)] $-\int p \ln p \leq - \int p \ln q$, that is, $h(U) \leq \mathbb{E}\Phi(U)$,
	\item[(b)] if $\mathbb{E} \Phi(U) \leq \mathbb{E} \Phi(V)$, then $h(U) \leq h(V)$.  
\end{itemize}
